{"toolCallId":"call_328cc8dddf9d483ab9672365d1d6515f","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"Add a threshold flag to the existing argument loop, validate it as an integer percentage, and gate the final exit status on the completion the run already reports. The report path is untouched.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.2 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2-compatible shell script, `set -euo pipefail` |\n| **Framework** | None - standalone CLI with a hand-rolled `case` argument loop |\n| **Storage** | None; per-run stats land in `${TMPDIR}/speckit-completeness-$$` and are removed by the `EXIT` trap |\n| **Testing** | vitest, `runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts`, driving the script through `spawnSync('bash', ...)` |\n\n### Overview\nThe script already computes the number the feature needs; what it lacks is a way to act on it. The change adds one branch to the existing argument loop, one validation block beside the other startup guards, and one gate between the report call and the final `exit 0`. No counting, formatting or JSON code is touched, which is what keeps the report byte-identical.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-file CLI with a linear pipeline: parse arguments, validate inputs, compute, report, then decide the exit status. Each stage already exists; the change extends three of them rather than adding a new structure.\n\n### Key Components\n- **Argument loop** (`§8`, currently lines 544-562): gains a `--min-percent` branch that consumes its value. `set -u` makes a bare trailing `--min-percent` an unbound-parameter abort, so the branch tests `$#` before reading `$2` and reports a usage error instead.\n- **Startup validation** (beside the existing `bc` check): rejects a value that is not an integer in 0-100. The integer test is `[[ \"$MIN_PERCENT\" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]`, which keeps the script's existing `[[ ]]` idiom and stays inside Bash 3.2.\n- **Reported-completion selector**: resolves which number the gate compares. Folder runs use `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE`; `--file` runs re-source the per-file stats and use `percentage`. The selector exists because `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` is initialised to `100` and only recomputed inside `calculate_spec_completeness`, so reading it in `--file` mode would compare against a constant and always pass.\n- **Exit gate** (after `output_text`/`output_json`, replacing the unconditional `exit 0`): compares the selected value against `MIN_PERCENT` and exits `3` when it is lower. Output has already been written at that point, so a failing run still prints its report.\n\n### Data Flow\n`argv` → argument loop → `MIN_PERCENT` (unset when the flag is absent) → validation → `calculate_file_completeness` or `calculate_spec_completeness` → `output_text` or `output_json` → gate → exit status.\n\n### Design Decisions\n\n| Decision | Why |\n|----------|-----|\n| Compare against the completion the run reports, not `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` unconditionally | `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` is `100` in `--file` mode because only the folder path recomputes it. Gating on it there would accept every file regardless of threshold - a silent no-op rather than a feature. |\n| Reject out-of-range values with exit `1` instead of clamping | Clamping `150` to `100` would report a threshold the caller never asked for. A usage error is honest and matches the script's existing exit-`1` behavior for bad arguments. |\n| Gate after the report, not before it | A threshold failure is still a successful measurement. Emitting the report first keeps `--min-percent` usable interactively and keeps stdout identical between a passing and a failing run. |\n| Exit `3` rather than `2` | `2` is reserved for validation errors in the sibling `spec/*.sh` scripts, and this script's own `1` already means user error. `3` is the system-error slot in that taxonomy but is unused here, so it carries the new meaning without colliding with either existing code. |\n| No short alias | The request names `--min-percent` only, and a single-letter alias risks colliding with a future option. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nExtend `runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts`, which already builds a temporary packet with a known placeholder count and spawns the script through `spawnSync`. New cases:\n\n| Case | Fixture | Assertion |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| Threshold met | Folder with one `[TODO: ...]` line, completion `C` | `--min-percent \"$C\"` exits `0` |\n| Threshold missed | Same folder | `--min-percent $((C + 1))` exits `3` |\n| Zero threshold | Same folder | `--min-percent 0` exits `0` |\n| Output stability | Same folder | stdout of a text run and of a `--json` run are identical with and without the flag |\n| Invalid input | None needed | `abc`, `-1`, `101` and a bare trailing flag each exit `1` |\n| Single-file mode | One file passed through `--file` | The per-file percentage decides the status, not a constant `100` |\n\nManual verification covers what the unit cases cannot: `bash -n` for syntax, and a `diff` of the full text and JSON reports from a before-and-after invocation.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n- `bc`, already required at startup for the percentage arithmetic. The new option adds no dependency.\n- Bash 3.2 or newer, the script's existing compatibility floor.\n- The vitest harness under `runtime/cli/tests/`, already present and already exercising this script.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\nRevert the two files named in `spec.md` §3. The option is additive and opt-in: a caller that never passes `--min-percent` sees no behavior change, so reverting the script alone restores the previous contract even if the tests are left in place.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"Add a threshold flag to the existing argument loop, validate it as an integer percentage, and gate the final exit status on the completion the run already reports. The report path is untouched.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.2 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2-compatible shell script, `set -euo pipefail` |\n| **Framework** | None - standalone CLI with a hand-rolled `case` argument loop |\n| **Storage** | None; per-run stats land in `${TMPDIR}/speckit-completeness-$$` and are removed by the `EXIT` trap |\n| **Testing** | vitest, `runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts`, driving the script through `spawnSync('bash', ...)` |\n\n### Overview\nThe script already computes the number the feature needs; what it lacks is a way to act on it. The change adds one branch to the existing argument loop, one validation block beside the other startup guards, and one gate between the report call and the final `exit 0`. No counting, formatting or JSON code is touched, which is what keeps the report byte-identical.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-file CLI with a linear pipeline: parse arguments, validate inputs, compute, report, then decide the exit status. Each stage already exists; the change extends three of them rather than adding a new structure.\n\n### Key Components\n- **Argument loop** (`§8`, currently lines 544-562): gains a `--min-percent` branch that consumes its value. `set -u` makes a bare trailing `--min-percent` an unbound-parameter abort, so the branch tests `$#` before reading `$2` and reports a usage error instead.\n- **Startup validation** (beside the existing `bc` check): rejects a value that is not an integer in 0-100. The integer test is `[[ \"$MIN_PERCENT\" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]`, which keeps the script's existing `[[ ]]` idiom and stays inside Bash 3.2.\n- **Reported-completion selector**: resolves which number the gate compares. Folder runs use `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE`; `--file` runs re-source the per-file stats and use `percentage`. The selector exists because `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` is initialised to `100` and only recomputed inside `calculate_spec_completeness`, so reading it in `--file` mode would compare against a constant and always pass.\n- **Exit gate** (after `output_text`/`output_json`, replacing the unconditional `exit 0`): compares the selected value against `MIN_PERCENT` and exits `3` when it is lower. Output has already been written at that point, so a failing run still prints its report.\n\n### Data Flow\n`argv` → argument loop → `MIN_PERCENT` (unset when the flag is absent) → validation → `calculate_file_completeness` or `calculate_spec_completeness` → `output_text` or `output_json` → gate → exit status.\n\n### Design Decisions\n\n| Decision | Why |\n|----------|-----|\n| Compare against the completion the run reports, not `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` unconditionally | `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` is `100` in `--file` mode because only the folder path recomputes it. Gating on it there would accept every file regardless of threshold - a silent no-op rather than a feature. |\n| Reject out-of-range values with exit `1` instead of clamping | Clamping `150` to `100` would report a threshold the caller never asked for. A usage error is honest and matches the script's existing exit-`1` behavior for bad arguments. |\n| Gate after the report, not before it | A threshold failure is still a successful measurement. Emitting the report first keeps `--min-percent` usable interactively and keeps stdout identical between a passing and a failing run. |\n| Exit `3` rather than `2` | `2` is reserved for validation errors in the sibling `spec/*.sh` scripts, and this script's own `1` already means user error. `3` is the system-error slot in that taxonomy but is unused here, so it carries the new meaning without colliding with either existing code. |\n| No short alias | The request names `--min-percent` only, and a single-letter alias risks colliding with a future option. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nExtend `runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts`, which already builds a temporary packet with a known placeholder count and spawns the script through `spawnSync`. New cases:\n\n| Case | Fixture | Assertion |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| Threshold met | Folder with one unfinished placeholder line, completion `C` | `--min-percent \"$C\"` exits `0` |\n| Threshold missed | Same folder | `--min-percent $((C + 1))` exits `3` |\n| Zero threshold | Same folder | `--min-percent 0` exits `0` |\n| Output stability | Same folder | stdout of a text run and of a `--json` run are identical with and without the flag |\n| Invalid input | None needed | `abc`, `-1`, `101` and a bare trailing flag each exit `1` |\n| Single-file mode | One file passed through `--file` | The per-file percentage decides the status, not a constant `100` |\n\nManual verification covers what the unit cases cannot: `bash -n` for syntax, and a `diff` of the full text and JSON reports from a before-and-after invocation.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n- `bc`, already required at startup for the percentage arithmetic. The new option adds no dependency.\n- Bash 3.2 or newer, the script's existing compatibility floor.\n- The vitest harness under `runtime/cli/tests/`, already present and already exercising this script.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\nRevert the two files named in `spec.md` §3. The option is additive and opt-in: a caller that never passes `--min-percent` sees no behavior change, so reverting the script alone restores the previous contract even if the tests are left in place.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_3b34ef65d371428094636f8209582a8d","title":"Read file","kind":"read","locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"read"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_407168ddcbb04384a6c8bf291d649dc8","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"[What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent\"\n  - \"add a min percent option to calculate completeness\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 1 |\n| **Priority** | [P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-completeness-min-percent` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n[What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- [Deliverable 1]\n- [Deliverable 2]\n- [Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [Excluded item 1] - [why]\n- [Excluded item 2] - [why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [path/to/file.js] | [Modify/Create/Delete] | [Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-001 | [Requirement description] | [How to verify it's done] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-002 | [Requirement description] | [How to verify it's done] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: [Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [System/API] | [What if blocked] | [Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [Risk description] | [High/Med/Low] | [Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 7. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- [Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"calculate-completeness.sh reports a completion percentage but always exits 0, so every caller that wants a threshold has to parse stdout. A --min-percent option moves that verdict into the exit status without touching the report.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent\"\n  - \"add a min percent option to calculate completeness\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 1 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-completeness-min-percent` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`calculate-completeness.sh` computes an overall completion percentage and prints it, then ends with an unconditional `exit 0` on the success path. A caller that wants to know whether a packet has reached some threshold has to capture stdout and re-parse either the human report or the `overall_completion` field of the JSON form. That parsing is repeated in every consumer, and it silently diverges whenever the report formatting changes.\n\n### Purpose\nAdd one option, `--min-percent <N>`, that turns the percentage the script already computes into an exit status: `3` below the threshold, `0` at or above it, with the report output left exactly as it is.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Accept `--min-percent <N>` in the existing argument-parsing loop and consume its value.\n- Validate `N` as an integer in the range 0-100, rejecting anything else as a usage error.\n- Exit `3` when the completion the run reports is below `N`, and `0` otherwise.\n- Document the option in `show_help` and add exit code `3` to the script's exit-code comment block.\n- Extend the existing vitest suite for the script with threshold, output-stability and validation cases.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- Changing how completion is computed - placeholder patterns, line counting, `bc` rounding and clamping all stay as they are, because the option only reads the value they produce.\n- Changing the report - neither the text layout nor the JSON schema gains a threshold field, and neither may drift.\n- Changing the meaning of existing exit codes - `0` and `1` keep their current triggers; `3` is additive.\n- Adding a short alias for the option - the request names `--min-percent` only.\n- Wiring the option into `archive.sh`, hooks or CI - those are separate changes with their own exit-code contracts.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh` | Modify | Add flag parsing, validation, the threshold gate, help text and exit-code documentation |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts` | Modify | Cover the pass case, the fail case, invalid input and unchanged output |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--min-percent <N>` is accepted and consumed with its value; the script exits `3` when the completion it reports is below `N` and `0` otherwise. | Against a folder whose reported completion is `C`: `--min-percent $((C + 1))` exits `3`, `--min-percent \"$C\"` exits `0`, `--min-percent 0` exits `0`. |\n| REQ-002 | The report output is unchanged with and without the option, in both text and `--json` form. | On the same folder, stdout of a `--min-percent` run is byte-identical to stdout of the same run without the flag; a `diff` of the two is empty. |\n| REQ-003 | An invalid `N` is a usage error: non-integer, negative, above 100, or a missing value each exit `1` with a message on stderr and no report. | `--min-percent abc`, `--min-percent -1`, `--min-percent 101` and a trailing bare `--min-percent` each exit `1`; stdout carries no report. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-004 | The gate runs after the report is written, so a failing run still prints its full report before exiting `3`. | An exit-`3` run emits the same complete report as an exit-`0` run. |\n| REQ-005 | Without the option the script behaves exactly as before, including exiting `0` on success. | The pre-existing assertions in `calculate-completeness-script.vitest.ts` pass unmodified. |\n| REQ-006 | `show_help` lists `--min-percent <N>` and the exit-code comment block records code `3`. | `--help` output names the option; the trailing comment block lists `3 - Completion below --min-percent`. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: A caller gates on a threshold with one invocation and no stdout parsing - exit `3` below `N`, `0` at or above `N`.\n- **SC-002**: Report output is byte-identical with and without the flag, for both the text and the JSON form.\n- **SC-003**: No existing exit path changes and the full vitest suite for the script passes.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Risk | `--file` mode has no Overall Completion - `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` is initialised to `100` and only recomputed by `calculate_spec_completeness`, so a gate reading it directly would pass every single-file run. | High - the option would silently do nothing in that mode. | Gate on the completion the run actually reports: `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` for a folder run, the sourced per-file `percentage` for a `--file` run. Covered by a dedicated test. |\n| Risk | A caller treats every non-zero exit as \"script broke\" rather than \"below threshold\". | Medium | Exit `3` only occurs when the caller passed the flag; help text and the header comment both name it. |\n| Risk | Adding the option to `show_help` changes `--help` output, which a strict output comparison could read as a regression. | Low | The stability requirement is scoped to the completeness report; help text must document a new option or it is undiscoverable. |\n| Dependency | `bc` for percentage arithmetic, already required at startup. | Low if present | Unchanged - the script already exits `1` with an install hint when `bc` is missing. |\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2 compatibility, already a stated constraint of the script. | Low | Use `[[ =~ ]]` and `if` blocks only; no `local -n`, no associative arrays. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 7. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\nNo blocking questions. Two behaviors the request leaves implicit are decided in `plan.md` §3 and carried by REQ-001 and REQ-002:\n\n- **`--file` mode.** The request names Overall Completion, which only a folder run computes. The gate compares against the completion the run reports, so `--file` runs compare their own per-file percentage rather than a value that is never computed.\n- **Help text.** \"Output unchanged\" is read as the completeness report. `show_help` gains the option and the header comment gains exit code `3`, since an undocumented option is unusable.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":34,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":20}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
